---
// NYHETER — the front page's news bar, at the foot of the opening package.
//
// Six notices from the last day, each a headline with its stamp under it, two to each
// of the page's thirds, under a hairline, with the way on to /nyheter/ in its head row:
// the grammar every section on the page opens with (rule, kicker row, content).
// It is run down with the eye in a few seconds, not read.
//
// SIX, AND SET LIKE THE PAGE'S OWN ITEMS (2026-10-11). It was three headlines on one
// line each, a clock and a headline. Three looked right and counted wrong: "i have
// never seen a site with that few items … either more or only one with a link to the
// news feed" (user review). A »senaste nytt« block is five or six rows wherever a
// Swedish reader has met one, so three read as a list cut short. One was the other
// way out, a single line with the way on; it was not taken because one headline of the
// fifty-five a day says nothing of the day, and the page's second half-title is the day.
// With six the clock could no longer stand in front of the headline: on a third of the
// canvas it left the headline 305px and one in ten was cut, every other visit with six
// on the page. So a notice is a headline and its stamp UNDER it, the order every other
// item on the page has, and the headlines stand on the page's three column lines
// (nyheter.css has the measurements and the breakpoints).
//
// WHERE IT STANDS, AND WHY (2026-10-10). It was a column of five under the kicker
// »Senaste nytt«, in a row with the Islamic year, 4 600px down a 7 400px page: the one
// thing on the page that goes stale by the hour stood in its last third, under a label
// that promised the opposite ("feels off … labeled like that and so far down", user
// review). News goes first on a front page because it is newest, and this news is not
// the site's own, so it gets the newest position at the smallest size: directly under
// the lead, in the register of a dateline. The lead and the essays keep the pictures
// and the serif.
//
// THE NAME. »Nyheter«, the name the destination has in the footer and as its h1
// (DESIGN.md, »Names«), and not »Senaste nytt«: twenty lines above it the lead's rail
// is headed »Senast publicerat«, and two labels that alike said two kinds of thing
// were one.
//
// Every headline links INWARD, to that notice on /nyheter/. The news is read on
// islam.se; zuhd.news is credited once here, beside the name, and once more on
// /nyheter/, and that is the whole of it. An earlier draft put an outbound link on
// every row, which turned the front page into six exits to a site the reader had
// never heard of.
//
// ⚠️ That credit IS the one outbound link, and it is meant to be. The rule above
// is one exit, not none: a provenance note that names a publication without
// letting the reader go and look at it is asking to be taken on trust.
//
// It renders a frame and nothing else: the rows arrive from
// `src/scripts/nyheter.ts`, which fetches zuhd's Swedish payload in the browser.
import SectionLink from "./SectionLink.astro";
import "../styles/nyheter.css";
---

<aside id="nyheter-rail" class="nyhetsremsa" aria-labelledby="nyheter-rail-rubrik" data-lage="vantar">
	<div class="nyhetsremsa-huvud">
		<h2 class="label home-kicker" id="nyheter-rail-rubrik">Nyheter</h2>
		<p class="nyheter-avsandare">
			<span class="nyheter-fran">från</span> <a href="https://zuhd.news/" rel="noopener">zuhd.news</a>
		</p>
		<SectionLink class="label home-head-all" href="/nyheter/">Alla nyheter</SectionLink>
	</div>
	<ol class="nyheter-lista" data-nyheter="rail" aria-busy="true"></ol>
</aside>

<script>
	// The bar is client-rendered — nothing on the front page is edge-injected,
	// because this is the site's most important document and it does not get a
	// fetch-dependent Function in front of it. Without this import the bar keeps
	// its place and never shows.
	import "../scripts/nyheter";
</script>
